const title = 'Untangling Mental Strongholds';
const standfirst = 'A recovery manual, step by step: five movements for bringing the thought that keeps winning under the obedience of Christ.';
const date = 'September 29, 2026';

const body = `Most of us know the thought before we can name it. It arrives on schedule: the old accusation when you fail, the fear that tightens at night, the voice that sounds so much like your own that you never think to question it. You have argued with it, prayed about it, tried to outrun it. Still it wins.

Scripture has a word for this. Paul calls it a *stronghold*: a fortified place in the mind, built from a lie and defended by long habit. He does not tell us to manage it or make peace with it. He tells us it can be pulled down.

+ For the weapons of our warfare are not carnal, but mighty through God to the pulling down of strong holds; casting down imaginations, and every high thing that exalteth itself against the knowledge of God, and bringing into captivity every thought to the obedience of Christ. — 2 Corinthians 10:4–5

What follows is not a formula and not a quick fix. It is a way of walking, five steps taken slowly and often taken again. Read it through once. Then come back with a notebook and take it one step at a time.

## Step 1: Name the stronghold

A stronghold is a lie that has hardened into a way of thinking. It rarely announces itself as a lie. It sounds like common sense, like realism, like the plain truth about you. *You always ruin things. God is tired of you. If people really knew you, they would leave.* Because it has been repeated so long, it no longer feels like a thought at all. It feels like the weather.

That is why the first step is simply to name it, plainly and without shame. You cannot pull down a wall you refuse to look at.

Take a notebook and write the thought down, word for word, exactly as it speaks to you. Do not soften it or tidy it up. Then, over the next few days, notice when it speaks loudest: the time of day, the place, the conversation or memory that sets it off. Patterns will begin to show.

Finally, ask three questions of what you have written. What does this thought claim about God? What does it claim about me? What does it claim about other people? Every stronghold makes a claim on all three, and seeing those claims written in your own hand is often the first loosening of its grip.

> You cannot pull down a wall you refuse to look at.

## Step 2: Bring it into the light

What stays hidden keeps its power. Strongholds depend on secrecy. They grow in the private room of the mind where no one else can answer them, and they tell us that if we ever spoke them aloud, we would be rejected.

So the second step is to break the secrecy, first before God and then before one other person.

Take what you wrote in Step 1 and bring it to God in prayer, out loud if you can. There is something about hearing your own voice say the lie that strips it of its mystery. Tell Him what it has claimed, how long it has been there, and how tired you are of it. He is not surprised. He has been waiting for you to bring it to Him.

Then choose one trusted, mature believer: a pastor, an elder, a friend who prays. Tell them what you have named. You do not need to tell them everything at once. Ask them to pray with you, and when they do, receive it. Let someone else's faith stand beside yours for a while.

+ If we walk in the light, as he is in the light, we have fellowship one with another, and the blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin. — 1 John 1:7

## Step 3: Renounce and replace

A lie is not only refused. It is replaced. If you tear down a wall and leave the ground empty, the old builders will come back. The place where the lie stood has to be filled with what God has actually said.

Begin by renouncing the lie by name, in the authority of Christ. This is not a magic phrase. It is a decision spoken aloud: *In the name of Jesus, I renounce the lie that I am beyond forgiving. I will not agree with it any longer.* Be specific. Name the exact words you wrote in Step 1.

Then find the passage of Scripture that answers that lie directly. If the lie says you are condemned, go to Romans 8:1. If it says you are alone, go to Hebrews 13:5. If it says God is finished with you, go to Philippians 1:6. Ask your trusted believer to help you look if you cannot find the words yourself.

Write that truth down and put it where the old thought used to win: on the bathroom mirror, inside the car, on the first page of your journal. When the lie speaks, answer it with the verse, out loud if you need to. Jesus answered every temptation in the wilderness the same way: *It is written.*

+ And ye shall know the truth, and the truth shall make you free. — John 8:32

## Step 4: Establish the new boundary

Freedom has to be guarded. A city whose walls have just been rebuilt does not leave its gates open at night. In the same way, the ground you have taken back needs boundaries, decided in advance and kept with the help of others.

Look back at the patterns you noticed in Step 1. Identify the voices, habits and places that fed the lie: the late-night scrolling, the friendship that always ends in comparison, the music, the old photographs, the silence after a hard phone call. Not everything on that list is sinful. Some of it is simply where you are weakest.

For each one, choose one concrete limit. Not a vague intention, but something you could check off: *phone in another room after ten*, *no visits to that page*, *a walk instead of the second glass*. Then tell someone what your limits are. A boundary no one else knows about is easy to quietly move.

Last, plan what you will do instead when the old pull returns, because it will. Decide now: *When it starts, I will read my verse aloud, text my friend, and step outside.* A plan made in peace is far stronger than a decision made in the moment.

+ Keep thy heart with all diligence; for out of it are the issues of life. — Proverbs 4:23

## Step 5: Keep watch

Strongholds rarely fall in a day. They were built one stone at a time, and they are usually taken down the same way. Some mornings you will feel completely free. On others the old thought will be back at the gate, and you will be tempted to believe nothing has changed. It has. Keep watch.

For the next forty days, return to your written truth every morning before anything else: read it, pray it, and ask God for the strength for that one day. Keep a short record of each victory, however small. *Tuesday: the thought came at lunch; I answered it; it went quiet.* Over weeks, that record becomes its own testimony, and on hard days you can read it back and see how far you have come.

Expect setbacks and do not let them become a new accusation. Falling back is not the same as going back. Simply return to Step 2, bring it into the light again, and keep walking.

And if the battle is bigger than you, if the thoughts are frightening, constant, or pulling you toward harming yourself, seek help without shame. Talk to your pastor, and see a doctor or a licensed counselor. God works through medicine and wise counsel as surely as through prayer. Reaching for help is not a failure of faith; very often it is faith.

+ Be sober, be vigilant; because your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour: whom resist stedfast in the faith. — 1 Peter 5:8–9

## A closing word

The thought that keeps winning is not the truest thing about you. It is old, it is loud, and it is used to being obeyed, but it is not stronger than the One who lives in you. Name it. Bring it into the light. Answer it with the truth. Guard the ground you take back. And keep watch, one morning at a time.

You will not walk this alone.

*This manual offers spiritual and practical guidance. It is not legal, medical or psychological advice. If you are in crisis, contact your local emergency services or a crisis line straight away.*`;
